migcom-64bit-host.patch
Licence: this patch changes files of Apple's bootstrap_cmds-72 (migcom.tproj) and is under that
release's licence (Apple Public Source License 2.0, APPLE_LICENSE in the release).

What: three changes that let migcom of bootstrap_cmds-72 run on a 64-bit Linux host.
  mig.c         drops the "#error migcom is not yet x86_64 compatible" guard.
  utils.c       SkipVFPrintf() works on a va_copy of its va_list. Callers hand the same va_list to
                it twice (left and right side of an assignment) and rely on a 32-bit ABI in which a
                va_list is passed by value; on x86-64 the first call would consume the arguments of
                the second, and migcom crashes while it writes the user code.
  mig_machine.h always uses i386/mig_machine.h: 4-byte alignment and padding of message fields,
                which is also the rule of the 32-bit ARM target. The original chooses by the HOST
                architecture and stops on any host that is not i386, x86_64 or ppc.
Why: build-headers.sh builds migcom on the build machine to generate the Mach interface headers
(mach/clock.h, mach/mach_port.h, mach/arm/task.h, ...). No change affects what migcom writes into a
header: the user headers it generates from xnu-1456.1.26's libsyscall .defs were compared with
those of the reference SDK and no object of the build differs (README.md).
Affects: no object directly; only the generated mig headers, which the "mig" rows of sources.tsv name.

--- a/migcom/mig.c
+++ b/migcom/mig.c
@@ -111,9 +111,6 @@
 
 // 11/12/08 - gab: <rdar://problem/6352179>
 // we should never be built as an x86_64 binary...
-#ifdef __LP64__
-#error "migcom is not yet x86_64 compatible."
-#endif
 
 extern int yyparse();
 static FILE *myfopen();
--- a/migcom/mig_machine.h
+++ b/migcom/mig_machine.h
@@ -1,10 +1,4 @@
 #if !defined(_MIG_MACHINE_H)
 #define _MIG_MACHINE_H
-#if defined(__i386__) || defined(__x86_64__)
 #include "i386/mig_machine.h"
-#elif defined(__ppc__) || defined(__ppc64__)
-#include "ppc/mig_machine.h"
-#else
-#error Architecture not supported
-#endif
 #endif
--- a/migcom/utils.c
+++ b/migcom/utils.c
@@ -640,8 +640,10 @@
  * and %f are recognized.
  */
 void
-SkipVFPrintf(FILE *file, register char *fmt, va_list pvar)
+SkipVFPrintf(FILE *file, register char *fmt, va_list pvar_in)
 {
+  va_list pvar;
+  va_copy(pvar, pvar_in);
   if (*fmt == 0)
     return; /* degenerate case */
   
